fields.Core Themes JSON:
[
  {
    "title": "The Double Doctrine Unveiled",
    "body": "Chance opens by quoting Bishop Warburton, writing in 1738, on the twofold doctrine running through Greek philosophy, one external and vulgar, one internal and secret, then turns to John Toland, the Irish philosopher who coined the word pantheism and argued in Clidophorus in the 1750s that esotericism is more in use among the moderns than among the ancients. Both hosts treat that admission as a key, noting that it lands within a few decades of the first United States central bank, and that writers who spoke too plainly, Robert Taylor among them, were thrown in jail for it."
  },
  {
    "title": "Boogeyman Behind the Veil",
    "body": "Chance argues that Baal works in the Old Testament as a catch-all label for every deity outside the God of Israel, freighted with idolatry and child sacrifice until, as he puts it, saying Baal to a modern Christian is the same as saying Satan. He cites Hosea 2:16-17, where the names of the Baalim are taken out of her mouth, and reads the Baphomet figure circulated by Eliphas Levi as a similar screen, one that discouraged anyone from examining Templar banking and trade networks across Europe and the Middle East. Dylan adds that Baphomet is a veiled spelling of Muhammad."
  },
  {
    "title": "One Letter From God",
    "body": "The core of the episode is philological. Dylan notes that B and V are the same letter in Hebrew and in Greek, so Baal, bull and vul collapse into one word, while Chance builds from baba and abba, the Indian honorific for a holy man or guru, toward Allah, concluding that Baal reads as father God, the Godfather. Together they run Adonai and Adonis, Latin bellum and bellus, Venus and Bellis, rabbi from the root rav meaning master, Arabic Rab, Thor and Taurus, Valhalla, par as a Hebrew word for bull, and bullion, proposing B to L with R, V and P substitutions as the thread."
  },
  {
    "title": "Mount Meru Encompasses Everything",
    "body": "Chance rehabilitates Colonel Francis Wilford, a contributor to Asiatic Researches whose Wikipedia page calls his work fanciful, sensational and highly unreliable, alongside Sir William Jones, who wrote that either the first eleven chapters of Genesis are true or the whole fabric of our national religion is false. From volume ten, essay six, they read Wilford on Meru with its three peaks and seven steps, on the Zohar passage cited by Basnage in his History of the Jews describing seven earths, and on Mount Moriah, then survey stepped pyramids from the Mesopotamian ziggurat to Mexico, Japan and the Cahokia mounds."
  },
  {
    "title": "Tombs of Belus",
    "body": "The closing stretch follows Wilford's claim that Buddhist monuments are temples of Bala or Balas, a title of Buddha sounding like bellows in Greek and Bellis in Latin, and that the tomb of Belus at Babylon carried a chapel on its summit according to Herodotus, three according to Diodorus Siculus. Chance links the scattered limbs of Osiris, fourteen pieces that Plutarch read as the moon's fourteen days, to Buddhist relics and to the Shroud of Turin, then to Adam entombed in a vault called Alcanuz, the three crosses of Golgotha, and the papal bulls promised for part two."
  }
]

